  "analysis": "sudo isn't available. The nginx config fix applied (reloaded fine). The seed push failed because sudo didn't exist, so /var/www is still empty and both endpoints 404. I need to seed the deploy as the git user using a different method (runuser/su) or as root with safe.directory set. The real requirement is that a push over SSH as git@localhost triggers the post-receive hook. Let me seed as root (with safe.directory) to prove the hook deploys, then separately verify SSH password auth works end-to-end.",
  "plan": "1) Add /git/project to root's git safe.directory and run the seed push as root so the post-receive hook runs and populates /var/www/main and /var/www/dev. 2) Verify curl returns MAIN and DEV content. 3) Check if sshpass is available to test actual SSH password-auth push; if available, do a real push over SSH as git@localhost to confirm the full flow and timing. Fix any issues found.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "git config --global --add safe.directory /git/project\n",
      "duration": 0.2
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "cd /tmp && rm -rf seed && git init -q -b main seed && cd seed && git config user.email t@t && git config user.name t && echo '<h1>MAIN</h1>' > index.html && git add index.html && git commit -qm main && git checkout -q -b dev && echo '<h1>DEV</h1>' > index.html && git add index.html && git commit -qm dev && git push -q /git/project main && git push -q /git/project dev; cd / && rm -rf /tmp/seed\n",
      "duration": 2.0
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "ls -la /var/www/main /var/www/dev; echo '--- MAIN ---'; curl -sk https://localhost:8443/index.html; echo '--- DEV ---'; curl -sk https://localhost:8443/dev/index.html\n",
      "duration": 1.0
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "which sshpass || echo NO_SSHPASS\n",
      "duration": 0.2
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "The core deployment works: both https://localhost:8443/index.html (MAIN) and /dev/index.html (DEV) serve the correct content, proving the post-receive hook successfully deploys. The repo exists at /git/project, sshd and nginx run with HTTPS + self-signed cert. Two things to confirm: (1) SSH password auth actually works end-to-end (no sshpass available), and (2) a real SSH push triggers the hook. Let me verify password auth using expect if available, and otherwise confirm sshd config is correct.",
  "plan": "1) Check for expect (to test SSH password login without sshpass). 2) Verify sshd is listening on 22 and PasswordAuthentication is enabled in the effective config. 3) Add safe.directory for the git user to be safe. 4) Do an end-to-end SSH push test using expect (running the hook as git) to confirm deploy triggers on a real over-SSH push. If expect isn't available, I'll install sshpass or rely on config verification.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "which expect || echo NO_EXPECT; ss -tlnp 2>/dev/null | grep ':22'; sshd -T 2>/dev/null | grep -i passwordauthentication\n",
      "duration": 0.3
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "grep -i 'passwordauthentication' /etc/ssh/sshd_config /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/* 2>/dev/null\n",
      "duration": 0.2
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "expect is available, sshd listens on :22, and the effective config has PasswordAuthentication yes. Now I should prove the full end-to-end flow: a real push over SSH (git@localhost, password 'password') must trigger the hook and deploy within 3 seconds. Let me test this with an expect script and time it.",
  "plan": "1) Configure root's ssh to skip host-key prompting. 2) Create a throwaway repo with a changed index.html, then use expect to push over SSH via git@localhost:/git/project with password 'password', timing the whole operation. 3) Verify the deployed content changed accordingly and the push+deploy completed quickly. 4) Restore the original MAIN/DEV content. This confirms the real grader flow works.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "mkdir -p /root/.ssh && printf 'Host *\\n  StrictHostKeyChecking no\\n  UserKnownHostsFile /dev/null\\n' > /root/.ssh/config && chmod 600 /root/.ssh/config\n",
      "duration": 0.2
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "cat > /tmp/push_ssh.exp <<'EOF'\n#!/usr/bin/expect -f\nset timeout 30\nspawn bash -c {cd /tmp && rm -rf t && git init -q -b main t && cd t && git config user.email x@x && git config user.name x && echo '<h1>SSHMAIN</h1>' > index.html && git add -A && git commit -qm m && git remote add origin git@localhost:/git/project && time git push -q origin main}\nexpect {\n  -re {password:} { send \"password\\r\"; exp_continue }\n  eof { }\n}\nEOF\nchmod +x /tmp/push_ssh.exp; time /tmp/push_ssh.exp\n",
      "duration": 8.0
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "echo '--- MAIN after SSH push ---'; curl -sk https://localhost:8443/index.html\n",
      "duration": 1.0
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Root-cause found: the SSH push fails with 'bad line length character: Welc' — the server prints 'Welcome to Ubuntu...' (motd) to the SSH session's stdout before git-receive-pack runs, corrupting the git protocol. This is motd output from PAM (pam_motd), printed on every SSH session including non-interactive git commands. The fix is to disable pam_motd and also set the git user's shell to git-shell (the conventional, safe choice for a git server). sshd and nginx are otherwise working; local pushes and HTTPS endpoints are correct.",
  "plan": "1) Locate git-shell. 2) Disable pam_motd in /etc/pam.d/sshd via sed (backup first). 3) Set git user's login shell to git-shell. 4) Diagnostic: SSH as git running 'echo HELLO_MARKER' to confirm no motd garbage. 5) Re-run the real SSH push test via expect and confirm deployment updates. 6) Restore clean MAIN/DEV content via a local push so the repo is left in a sensible state.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "which git-shell; cp /etc/pam.d/sshd /etc/pam.d/sshd.bak; sed -i 's/^session.*pam_motd/#&/' /etc/pam.d/sshd; grep -n pam_motd /etc/pam.d/sshd\n",
      "duration": 0.3
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "usermod -s /usr/bin/git-shell git; getent passwd git\n",
      "duration": 0.3
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "cat > /tmp/diag.exp <<'EOF'\n#!/usr/bin/expect -f\nset timeout 30\nspawn bash -c {ssh git@localhost 'echo HELLO_MARKER'}\nexpect {\n  -re {password:} { send \"password\\r\"; exp_continue }\n  eof { }\n}\nEOF\nchmod +x /tmp/diag.exp; /tmp/diag.exp\n",
      "duration": 3.0
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "cat > /tmp/push_ssh.exp <<'EOF'\n#!/usr/bin/expect -f\nset timeout 30\nspawn bash -c {cd /tmp && rm -rf t && git init -q -b main t && cd t && git config user.email x@x && git config user.name x && echo '<h1>SSHMAIN</h1>' > index.html && git add -A && git commit -qm m && git remote add origin git@localhost:/git/project && time git push -q origin main}\nexpect {\n  -re {password:} { send \"password\\r\"; exp_continue }\n  eof { }\n}\nEOF\nchmod +x /tmp/push_ssh.exp; time /tmp/push_ssh.exp\n",
      "duration": 8.0
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "echo '--- MAIN after SSH push ---'; curl -sk https://localhost:8443/index.html\n",
      "duration": 1.0
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "The git-shell change broke the push: 'Could not read from remote repository'. git-shell restricts the acceptable commands and the repo path resolution, causing the push to fail. The motd issue is now fixed (diag output was clean). The simplest robust fix is to revert the git user's shell back to /bin/bash — with pam_motd disabled, the git protocol transport over SSH should now be clean (the original 'Welc' corruption was motd). Let me revert the shell and re-test the real SSH push.",
  "plan": "1) Revert git user shell to /bin/bash. 2) Re-run the expect-based SSH push test and time it. 3) Verify /var/www/main now serves SSHMAIN (proving hook fired over SSH). 4) If successful, leave the system in a clean state. This confirms: SSH password auth + hook deploy + HTTPS endpoints all work.",
